well we can get some immediate reaction  from the nurses Union the women that  we've been talking about a little bit in  that interview uh the Royal College of  Nursing Pat Cullen and the union says  that its members will be walking out  tomorrow and it wants direct pay  negotiations with the government well  let's speak now shall we too uh the  general secretary and chief executive  Pat Cullen thank you very much for being  with me uh today uh we've just heard  from Maria Caulfield who says that the  strikes aren't really about pay they're  only a smaller element about paying it's  more about conditions is that right  so that's an absolutely not true and I  heard Maria describe  um and quite a disparaging way pay has  just been a tiny thing for our nursing  staff our nursing staff are on the  breadline they're not able to afford  their bills there are some of them  aren't even able to afford travel to get  to their work for goodness sake and yet  we have a minister sitting here saying  that it's about all of the other things  and not about pay that is absolutely not  true of course there are many other  terms and conditions we would like this  government to sort out for our nurses  absolutely but pay is fundamental if we  are going to try and keep the nurses  we've got in the Health Service and try  and recruit more than 50 000 vacancies  in this country and if we don't address  pay then I'm afraid I most nurses would  say to you tonight they are fearful for  where the Health Service will end up  right let's talk about pay then shall we  let's talk about numbers  um you open negotiations asking for 19  at pay rise at five percent above  inflation the government said that's  unaffordable what would you accept  I said we'd get around a table and talk  about this uh but I heard you mention  about the meeting that I had with um  Steve Barkley a few days back and once  again brought into a room and told you  can talk about anything you wish to talk  to me about but we will not be talking  about pay and until I can get into a  room and talk about pay to the ministers  then I'm afraid I'm not going to  negotiate for the hundreds of thousands  of nurses that will be on picket lines  tomorrow over the airwaves but they owe  those nurses  um everything to get into a room and  start to talk about pay surely to  goodness that's the only respectful  thing they could do at this stage  instead or instead of what they're doing  they are allowing those hundreds of  thousands of nurses to actually go on to  picket lines tomorrow and stand out in  the cold when they want to be inside  looking after their patients but this is  kind of the point though isn't it  because you've said yourself that the  health secretary isn't talking to you  about numbers  um and yet the government is making a  huge play that fact that you want a 19  pay rise they clearly feel that that  plays into their hands that most people  listening will think it's unaffordable  so surely this is your opportunity to  give a bit of guidance on what you would  accept so in Scotland for example uh  there's been an accepted offer of 7.5  would you accept that  look we had an opportunity to stop the  spin and start to speak a few days ago I  was very optimistic going to that  meeting hoping that at least we would  have some sense shown  um and then we could actually have  stopped the strike tomorrow but in fact  that was never going to be on the cards  for this government they brought us into  a room they marched us up to the top of  the hill again and they abandoned our  nursing staff the 320 000 nurses that  have voted for for strike action that's  an incredible and significant mandate  and you cannot ignore their voices you  absolutely cannot they're not asking for  uh as I keep saying their pockets to be  lined with gold they're wanting a decent  decent wage that isn't they're entitled  to a decent wage and if we are going to  try and address the issues within our  Health Service we need to retain our  nurses that we've got in the Health  Service those nurses that Maria talked  about today on your program that are not  able to get their annual leave they're  not able they're working every R that's  available to them to keep the Health  Service going and actually when you talk  about the 19 we never asked for 19  in February of this year we submitted  our evidence to the peer review body and  at that point inflation was not sitting  where it is now and then in July uh the  the government accepted the  recommendation of the pay-per-view body  I never accepted the recommendations nor  did our nurses and inflation wasn't  where it is now either and so the more  they've tried to release the longer  they've tried their helis and Fletcher  has continued to rise and consequently  we're sitting at 19 but what I would say  to Maria and to Steve Barkley and any  other minister listening can you afford  not to do this and indeed the  international Council of nurses wrote to  Steve Bartley this evening and told them  plainly and squarely that he cannot  afford not to do it either so you're  saying you never asked for 19 just this  is your chance though to clear it up and  I know you know I understand what you're  saying about you know you don't want to  negotiate on the Airways but I do feel  as well that you know nurses are going  on strike the public have a right to  know exactly what it is you're striking  for I just don't understand why you  won't be a bit more specific on what  kind of pay rise you think would be you  know Fair  no I'm definitely not going to negotiate  on the airwaves and I don't think that  any Minister should reduce or a  profession to have to do that they  should they should do the decent thing  and get into a room and negotiate with  me I've also said if they find that that  maybe isn't isn't suitable for them that  we will do it through conciliation and  engage with aircast I don't think that's  very much for our profession to ask and  I don't think that any Minister should  suggest or ask that we would negotiate  on Airways and not give us the respect  and get into a room with us uh okay  um if the government doesn't uh do what  you're asking to you get around the  table and negotiate on pay should we  expect to see more nurses strikes  well the the  um the balls in their Court quite  frankly uh there will be a second day on  the 20th of December and unless we have  um talks and unless we are able to  negotiate on behalf of my members then  I'm afraid to say that that's that's a  very strong possibility and already we  will be starting to look at when those  deaths will be  so what kind of time frame would be  you're saying you'll be looking at what  when that could be what kind of time  frame are we looking at then potentially  I'm afraid that they will continue into  January okay that's a really interesting  uh to hear uh certainly uh something uh  sobering as well if you look at the  current waiting times operations in the  NHS and what do you think the impacts of  the strikes will be  well just when you mentioned waiting  lists I think that's a really important  point  um why are those nurses going on picket  lines tomorrow and losing a day's pay  out of the meager salaries that they  that this government has  um insisted that they live on that they  can't live on actually  uh but our waiting lists are at 7.2  million uh people in this country  waiting year on year and waiting lists  languishing on waiting lists and why are  they because we haven't got the nurses  to be able to care and treat those  patients so the ministers will continue  to talk about if we take money from  um the elective care waiting lists and  and give it to the nurses then we won't  be able to attract and address the  elective care waiting lists but you'll  address new waiting lists if you do not  have the nursing staff to actually care  and treat those passions they're  actually one of the same thing so this  is again all part of the spin that's put  on this about taking from patients and  given to nurses it's nurses that look  after patients and without nursing staff  patients in this country are getting a  pretty raw day sitting on waiting list  year after year when their treatments  should be given to them at a time when  they require them and then they can get  on and live their fruitful and normal  lives and and continue to contribute to  society when in fact because of the ill  health and the increases in the real  health and their chronic conditions they  can't do that so everything leads back  everything leads back to paying nurses a  decent wage and ensuring we can recruit  staff into the Health Service and retain  those nurses we've got I was quite  struck by some comments that you made  after the meeting with Steve Barkley you  said that you thought the government was  displaying particularly Metro Behavior  towards the Royal College of Nursing  because it sees nursing as female work  and doesn't value it properly do you  think there is some sexism in the way  that nurses are treated  I absolutely do and in fact again I  refer back to the international Council  and for nurses and they have addressed  that in the letter to Steve Barkley this  evening as well so it's a well-known  fact that nursing staff is at 90 female  profession  and if you think of some of the things  that this government has um done over  this last number of weeks uh I have to  ask  um them would they do that if those if  those statistics were different if we  were not a 90 female profession  so just one example where they have  decided that the military can come in  and uh with a few hours of training and  take on the role of a nurse take on the  role of caring for patients and there's  not a single nurse in this country would  suggest that with a few hours training  that they could go out and put on a  military uniform and take a gun and do  what our military does because they  absolutely could not and but yet it  seems to be that the value of caring is  brought down to the lowest common  denominator and it can be done by anyone  with a few hours training and that's  just so wrong if you look at what the  government has done with saying they're  going to put in more Draconian  legislation to ensure that through their  trade unions nursing cannot have a voice  that's about trying to silence those  nurses the 90 90 female profession that  we are and I could go on there's many  more examples that I could use but I'm  not alone in thinking this then  International currency of nurses believe  it as well and indeed the people that I  represent believe it

